WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.113
A ball falls through a modest
height with negligible drag.

00:00:03.765 --> 00:00:07.324
What happens to its gravitational
potential and kinetic energy?

00:00:08.033 --> 00:00:12.757
Gravitational potential energy decreases
while kinetic energy increases.

00:00:13.309 --> 00:00:16.853
If drag is significant, some
energy is transferred to internal

00:00:16.854 --> 00:00:18.647
stores of the air and ball too.

00:00:19.375 --> 00:00:19.968
Correct.

00:00:20.413 --> 00:00:25.253
When it hits the ground, deformation and
vibrations transfer energy into elastic

00:00:25.253 --> 00:00:27.380
and internal stores and through sound.

00:00:27.806 --> 00:00:32.211
A bounce can return some energy to kinetic
and gravitational potential stores.

00:00:33.023 --> 00:00:36.131
If the next bounce is lower,
energy has not vanished.

00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:39.742
Less remains available in the ball's
mechanical stores because more

00:00:39.753 --> 00:00:41.384
has transferred to other places.

00:00:42.187 --> 00:00:43.763
Now a wind-up toy.

00:00:44.322 --> 00:00:48.764
Where is energy stored after winding,
and where does it go when the toy runs?

00:00:49.553 --> 00:00:51.900
In its strained spring's elastic store.

00:00:52.327 --> 00:00:56.872
During release, energy transfers to
the moving toy's kinetic store and

00:00:57.071 --> 00:01:01.329
through friction and other processes
to internal stores of the surroundings.

00:01:02.163 --> 00:01:02.509
Good.

00:01:03.012 --> 00:01:06.768
For a rollercoaster, gravitational
and kinetic energy exchange

00:01:06.781 --> 00:01:08.290
as it travels down and up.

00:01:08.685 --> 00:01:12.500
With resistance, the mechanical
energy of the coaster-Earth system

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:16.635
decreases while total energy,
including surroundings, is conserved.

00:01:17.373 --> 00:01:21.194
Saying total energy is conserved
except for friction is wrong.

00:01:21.644 --> 00:01:23.859
Friction changes where energy goes.

00:01:24.087 --> 00:01:26.129
It does not break the conservation rule.

00:01:26.706 --> 00:01:27.474
Exactly.

00:01:27.715 --> 00:01:31.531
That sentence has rescued a whole
box of imaginary teacher tissues.

00:01:31.964 --> 00:01:35.796
Always say which stores or system
you mean before using the word lost.

00:01:36.166 --> 00:01:39.047
Is energy transferred by
heating always wasted?

00:01:39.831 --> 00:01:40.127
No.

00:01:40.525 --> 00:01:43.249
In a room heater, warming
the room is the purpose.

00:01:43.717 --> 00:01:47.269
In a motor meant to lift a load,
unwanted heating may reduce the

00:01:47.271 --> 00:01:48.932
fraction transferred usefully.

00:01:49.724 --> 00:01:50.562
Exactly.

00:01:50.905 --> 00:01:55.263
Useful and wasted depend on the
intended task and the chosen boundary.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:59.982
A lamp is meant to provide useful
illumination, while unwanted heating

00:01:59.993 --> 00:02:01.907
usually does not serve that purpose.

00:02:02.676 --> 00:02:04.702
Wasted energy is still conserved.

00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:08.831
It often dissipates into internal
stores spread throughout the

00:02:08.833 --> 00:02:09.594
surroundings.

00:02:10.359 --> 00:02:10.930
Correct.

00:02:11.289 --> 00:02:14.922
Once energy is spread through the
environment at nearly uniform temperature,

00:02:15.228 --> 00:02:18.940
it is difficult to collect and turn
back into the particular useful task.

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:22.791
Conservation does not mean every
transfer is easily reversible.

00:02:23.512 --> 00:02:27.840
Bearings or lubrication reduce some
friction, streamlining reduces drag,

00:02:28.002 --> 00:02:30.354
and insulation slows unwanted heating.

00:02:30.847 --> 00:02:33.797
But friction is useful for
brakes and grip, so removing it

00:02:33.813 --> 00:02:35.379
everywhere would be a bad plan.

00:02:36.015 --> 00:02:36.806
Exactly.

00:02:37.211 --> 00:02:41.311
Engineering asks which transfers
help the purpose and which do not.

00:02:41.898 --> 00:02:46.346
A quieter motor may waste less energy
through sound, but sound alone does

00:02:46.347 --> 00:02:48.281
not tell us its overall efficiency.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.061
We need measurements of total input
and useful output, not just an

00:02:53.062 --> 00:02:55.371
impressive noise or a shiny casing

00:02:55.763 --> 00:03:00.659
A motor receives 200 joules and
transfers 150 joules usefully.

00:03:00.921 --> 00:03:05.949
Find its efficiency and the remaining
energy transfer Useful energy divided

00:03:05.949 --> 00:03:10.913
by total input gives 150 over 200, 0.75

00:03:11.177 --> 00:03:12.591
or 75%.

00:03:13.843 --> 00:03:16.945
50 joules goes elsewhere Correct.

00:03:17.528 --> 00:03:20.337
Efficiency is a ratio, so it has no unit.

00:03:20.705 --> 00:03:23.824
Multiply the fraction
by 100 for a percentage.

00:03:24.305 --> 00:03:26.719
Do not confuse 0.75

00:03:26.719 --> 00:03:28.626
with 0.75%.

00:03:29.422 --> 00:03:32.935
A Sankey diagram shows the
accounting using arrow widths.

00:03:33.323 --> 00:03:36.160
The two outgoing widths
add to the incoming width.

00:03:36.537 --> 00:03:41.878
The useful branch is three times as
wide as the wasted branch here Exactly.

00:03:42.186 --> 00:03:43.963
Labels and scale matter.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:49.110
An efficiency above 100% for a simple
energy conversion suggests missing

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:54.523
input energy, inconsistent boundaries,
or a calculation error But can a heater

00:03:54.542 --> 00:03:59.670
be nearly 100% efficient at turning
electricity into heating indoors?

00:04:00.414 --> 00:04:03.307
Yes, for that narrowly defined purpose.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.867
It is too absolute to say
no device can ever be 100%

00:04:07.867 --> 00:04:09.694
efficient under any definition.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:14.512
Practical systems still need careful
boundaries and a clear, useful outcome

00:04:15.388 --> 00:04:19.766
A heat pump can deliver more heating
energy than its electrical input because

00:04:19.768 --> 00:04:22.117
it also transfers energy from outside.

00:04:22.646 --> 00:04:26.193
That is not creating energy Correct.

00:04:26.533 --> 00:04:30.220
Its performance ratio has a different
definition from the simple conversion

00:04:30.222 --> 00:04:31.921
efficiency we just calculated.

00:04:32.447 --> 00:04:36.671
Count the environmental energy input
too, and conservation still works.

00:04:37.475 --> 00:04:40.000
Bonus distinction, power is
energy transferred per second.
